The final match of the [[2026 FIFA World Cup]], the 23rd edition of [[FIFA]]'s competition for national [[Association football|soccer]] teams, was played at [[MetLife Stadium]] in [[East Rutherford, New Jersey]], part of the [[New York metropolitan area]], on July 19, 2026, and was contested by [[Spain national football team|Spain]] and defending champions [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]].    

The tournament was contested by hosts [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]], [[Mexico national football team|Mexico]], and [[Canada men's national soccer team|Canada]], and 45 other teams who emerged from [[2026 FIFA World Cup qualification|the qualification phase]], which was organized by the six [[List of football federations#Continental|FIFA confederations]]. The 48 teams competed in a group stage, from which 32 teams qualified for the [[2026 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|knockout stage]]. En route to the final, Spain finished first in [[2026 FIFA World Cup Group H|Group H]] with two wins and a draw, before defeating [[Austria national football team|Austria]] in the round of 32, [[Portugal national football team|Portugal]] in the round of 16, [[Belgium national football team|Belgium]] in the quarterfinal, and [[France national football team|France]] in the semifinal. Argentina finished first in [[2026 FIFA World Cup Group J|Group J]] with three wins, after which they defeated [[Cape Verde national football team|Cape Verde]] in the round of 32, [[Egypt national football team|Egypt]] in the round of 16, [[Switzerland national football team|Switzerland]] in the quarterfinal, and [[England national football team|England]] in the semifinal. The final took place in front of 80,663 spectators, and was [[Referee (association football)|refereed]] by [[Slavko Vinčić]] of Slovenia. 

[[Lamine Yamal]] had a chance to score for Spain early in the first half when he hit a curl shot, which was saved by Argentina's [[Goalkeeper (association football)|goalkeeper]] [[Emiliano Martínez]], and [[Marc Cucurella]] had an opportunity to score shortly before half-time but his shot went wide of the goal. In the second half, [[Ferran Torres]] had a chance when he met with a [[Header (association football)|header]], but his shot was denied by Martínez. Before the end of [[Glossary of association football terms#Regulation time|regulation time]], Argentina midfielder [[Enzo Fernández]] received a second yellow card for a [[Glossary of association football terms#Challenge|challenge]] against [[Pau Cubarsí]] and was [[Penalty card#Red card|sent off]]; shortly afterwards, Yamal took a [[free kick]], but his shot was saved by Martínez. With the match goalless after 90 minutes—and with Argentina becoming the first team in a World Cup final to register no shots in regulation time—the game went into [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]], in the second period of which Spain broke the deadlock. Torres, who had come on as a [[Substitute (association football)|substitute]], received the ball from a header by [[Nico Williams]] and kicked it with a left-footed shot to secure a 1–0 victory for Spain. After the final whistle, a brawl broke out, in which multiple Argentina players were involved.

Spain's win was their [[List of FIFA World Cup finals|second World Cup title]] and the first since [[2010 FIFA World Cup final|2010]], becoming the first country to simultaneously hold the men's and women's World Cup titles, having won the latter in [[2023 FIFA Women's World Cup|2023]]. Torres was named the [[man of the match]]. The match saw the most saves ever in a World Cup final, with 11 by Argentina goalkeeper [[Emiliano Martínez]]. The match was preceded by the closing ceremony. The match was the first ever World Cup final to include a [[2026 FIFA World Cup final halftime show|halftime show]], which caused the full halftime break to last over 27 minutes, against [[International Football Association Board]] (IFAB) protocol.

==Background==
[[File:P20260719DT-1368 President Donald J. Trump and First Lady Melania Trump attend the FIFA World Cup Final.jpg|thumb|The ''Trionda Final'' ball used in the final.]]

The [[2026 FIFA World Cup]] was the 23rd edition of the [[FIFA World Cup|World Cup]], [[FIFA]]'s [[association football|soccer]] competition for national teams, held in Canada, Mexico and the United States between June 11 and July 19, 2026.<ref name=":1">{{cite news|title=FIFA World Cup 2026: Fixtures, results, group standings|url=https://www.reuters.com/graphics/SOCCER-WORLDCUP/mopaoglqkpa/|work=[[Reuters]]|accessdate=July 17, 2026|archive-date=July 18, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260718151218/https://www.reuters.com/graphics/SOCCER-WORLDCUP/mopaoglqkpa/|url-status=live}}</ref> The national teams of [[Canada men's national soccer team|Canada]], [[Mexico national football team|Mexico]], and the [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]] automatically qualified for the tournament as hosts, while 206 teams competed for the remaining 45 slots through [[2026 FIFA World Cup qualification|qualifying rounds]] organized by the six [[List of football federations#Continental|FIFA confederations]] and held between September 2023 and March 2026.{{efn|Attributed to multiple references:<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{cite news|date=October 8, 2025|title=What teams are qualified for 2026 World Cup? Full list, latest updates|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2025/10/08/world-cup-2026-qualified-teams-soccer/86584914007/|accessdate=July 17, 2026|last=Anderson|first=Jason|work=[[USA Today]]|archive-date=October 11, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251011215832/https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2025/10/08/world-cup-2026-qualified-teams-soccer/86584914007/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|date=September 3, 2025|title=FIFA World Cup 2026 qualifying: All you need to know|url=https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/worldcup/canadamexicousa2026/articles/road-to-world-cup-26-qualifiers-usa-canada-mexico|publisher=FIFA|accessdate=July 17, 2026|archive-date=March 21, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250321160145/https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/worldcup/canadamexicousa2026/articles/road-to-world-cup-26-qualifiers-usa-canada-mexico/|url-status=live}}</ref>}} The tournament featured 48 teams for the first time, up from 32 in the [[2022 FIFA World Cup|2022 World Cup]].<ref name=":0">{{cite news|last=Dubow|first=Josh|date=June 7, 2026|title=World Cup by the numbers: 104 matches, 48 teams and 3 countries make this the largest ever|url=https://apnews.com/article/world-cup-numbers-4220a25c3efb04fc59c15b4d081556d9|accessdate=July 17, 2026|work=[[Associated Press News]]|archive-date=July 18, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260718162602/https://apnews.com/article/world-cup-numbers-4220a25c3efb04fc59c15b4d081556d9|url-status=live}}</ref> The teams were divided into twelve groups of four, with each team playing every other team once in a [[Round-robin tournament|round-robin format]]. The top two teams from each group, alongside the eight highest-ranked third-place teams, advanced to the [[2026 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|knockout stage]].<ref>{{cite news|last=McKessy|first=Jack|date=June 25, 2026|title=How does the World Cup work? Rules, everything to know about 2026 tournament|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2026/06/25/world-cup-2026-explained-what-to-know/90696906007/|work=USA Today|accessdate=July 17, 2026|archive-date=July 18, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260718054101/https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2026/06/25/world-cup-2026-explained-what-to-know/90696906007/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Som|first=Nicholas|date=May 13, 2026|title=World Cup group stage explained: Tiebreakers, third-place teams, more|url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/48703925/world-cup-group-stage-explained-tiebreakers-third-place-teams|publisher=[[ESPN]]|accessdate=July 17, 2026|archive-date=July 18, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260718145742/https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/48703925/world-cup-group-stage-explained-tiebreakers-third-place-teams|url-status=live}}</ref> 

[[Spain national football team|Spain]] had won the title once before, defeating [[Netherlands national football team|the Netherlands]] in the [[2010 FIFA World Cup final|2010 final]] in [[Johannesburg]], South Africa.<ref>{{cite news |last=Reineking |first=Jim |date=July 14, 2026 |title=Has Spain ever won a World Cup? |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2026/07/14/spain-world-cup-wins/90910423007/ |work=USA Today |accessdate=July 14, 2026 |archive-date=July 15, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260715074115/https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2026/07/14/spain-world-cup-wins/90910423007/ |url-status=live }}</ref> They were the reigning [[UEFA European Championship|UEFA European]] champions, having won their fourth title in [[UEFA Euro 2024 final|2024]]. Entering the 2026 final, Spain tied with [[Italy national football team|Italy]] for the [[List of world association football records#Longest streaks|longest unbeaten streak]] in the history of men's international soccer with 37 matches.<ref>{{cite news |last=Gitto |first=Andrew |date=July 16, 2026 |title=Spain unbeaten streak record: Inside La Roja's record-tying 37-match run without a loss since 2024 |url=https://www.sportingnews.com/us/soccer/news/spain-unbeaten-streak-record-inside-record-37-match-2024/726212e1dda1dced7763d54f |work=[[The Sporting News]] |accessdate=July 17, 2026 |archive-date=July 15, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260715111258/https://www.sportingnews.com/us/soccer/news/spain-unbeaten-streak-record-inside-record-37-match-2024/726212e1dda1dced7763d54f |url-status=live }}</ref> [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] were the defending [[2022 FIFA World Cup final|champions]] from the [[2022 FIFA World Cup|2022 World Cup]]. They had won the title three times in [[1978 FIFA World Cup|1978]], [[1986 FIFA World Cup|1986]] and 2022. They had also finished as runners-up three times, in [[1930 FIFA World Cup|1930]], [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990]] and [[2014 FIFA World Cup|2014]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Reineking |first=Jim |date=July 15, 2026 |title=How many World Cups has Argentina won? History beckons for Lionel Messi |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2026/07/15/argentina-world-cup-wins-all-time-messi/90923499007/ |work=USA Today |accessdate=July 17, 2026 |archive-date=July 16, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260716125741/https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/worldcup/2026/07/15/argentina-world-cup-wins-all-time-messi/90923499007/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The team was also the reigning [[Copa América]] champions, having won their 16th title in [[2024 Copa América|2024]].<ref>{{cite news |date=July 14, 2024 |title=Argentina edge Colombia to win record 16th Copa América title |url=https://abcnews.com/Sports/argentina-edge-colombia-win-record-16th-copa-amrica/story?id=111943591 |work=[[ABC News (United States)|ABC News]] |agency=ESPN News Services |accessdate=July 17, 2026}}</ref> Spain and Argentina had played each other fourteen times since 1952, with both teams winning six matches. They had met once at the World Cup, in the group stage of the [[1966 FIFA World Cup|1966 tournament]], which Argentina won 2–1. Their most recent meeting before the 2026 World Cup was a [[Exhibition game|friendly]] match in [[Madrid]] on March 27, 2018, which Spain won 6–1.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/spain/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Argentina/ |title=Spain national football team: record v Argentina |work=11v11.com |publisher=[[Association of Football Statisticians]] |accessdate=July 14, 2026 |archive-date=July 16, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260716015108/https://www.11v11.com/teams/spain/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Argentina/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Spain and Argentina were originally scheduled to meet at the [[2026 Finalissima]] in Qatar in March 2026. The match, however, was cancelled due to the [[2026 Iran war|Iran war]] and an inability to agree on a new venue.<ref>{{cite news |last=Kallas |first=Fernando |date=July 17, 2026 |title=Messi's Argentina face Spain's cool machine in World Cup final rich with personal twists |url=https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/messis-argentina-face-spains-cool-machine-world-cup-final-rich-with-personal-2026-07-17/ |work=Reuters |accessdate=July 17, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=March 15, 2026 |title=Argentina vs. Spain Finalissima in Qatar called off after widening of Middle East war |url=https://www.cbc.ca/sports/soccer/spain-argentina-qatar-soccer-finalissima-cancelled-9.7129565 |work=[[CBC Sports]] |agency=Reuters |accessdate=July 17, 2026}}</ref>

The 2026 final was the first time in the tournament's history to feature two reigning continental champions, who were the two top-ranked teams in the [[FIFA World Ranking]].<ref name="Athletic-Everything">{{cite news |last1=Bushnell |first1=Henry |last2=Carey |first2=Mark |last3=Anzidei |first3=Melanie |last4=Stanton |first4=John |display-authors=etal |date=July 17, 2026 |title=World Cup final 2026: Everything you need to know |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7446983/2026/07/17/world-cup-final-everything-you-need-to-know/ |work=[[The Athletic]] |url-access=subscription |accessdate=July 17, 2026}}</ref> It was also the second that two [[Spanish language|Spanish]]-speaking countries competed against each other, after [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]] and Argentina contested the inaugural [[1930 FIFA World Cup final|1930 final]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Rey |first=Débora |date=July 17, 2026 |title=Argentines and Spaniards face divided loyalties ahead of Spain-Argentina World Cup final |url=https://www.orlandosentinel.com/2026/07/17/argentina-spain-brotherhood/ |work=[[Orlando Sentinel]] |agency=Associated Press |accessdate=July 17, 2026 |archive-date=July 18, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260718021250/https://www.orlandosentinel.com/2026/07/17/argentina-spain-brotherhood/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Hodge |first=Mark |date=July 17, 2026 |title=From The Sports Desk: A blockbuster World Cup Final |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/sports/soccer/sports-newsletter-world-cup-final-rcna587988 |url-access=subscription |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260717214915/https://www.nbcnews.com/sports/soccer/sports-newsletter-world-cup-final-rcna587988 |archive-date=July 17, 2026 |access-date=July 20, 2026 |publisher=[[NBC News]] }}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Goldstein |first=Steven Louis |date=July 19, 2026 |title=World Cup final 2026: What U.S. viewers need to know to watch Spain vs. Argentina |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7452672/2026/07/19/world-cup-final-spain-argentina-yamal-messi-tv/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260720005623/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7452672/2026/07/19/world-cup-final-spain-argentina-yamal-messi-tv/ |archive-date=July 20, 2026 |access-date=July 20, 2026 |work=[[The Athletic]]|issn=0362-4331}}</ref> The official match ball, used in the semifinals, match for third place, and the final, was announced on July 6, 2026. It was a variation of the [[Adidas Trionda]] named the ''Trionda Final''. While the technical specifications remain the same, it had a special design with a gold, white, and black color scheme that resembles the [[FIFA World Cup Trophy]].<ref>{{cite press release |date=July 6, 2026 |title=Final four World Cup 2026 matches to be played with Trionda Final Official Match Ball |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/worldcup/canadamexicousa2026/articles/trionda-final-official-match-ball-final-four-matches |publisher=[[FIFA]] |accessdate=July 17, 2026 |archive-date=July 12, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260712091130/https://www.fifa.com/en/tournaments/mens/worldcup/canadamexicousa2026/articles/trionda-final-official-match-ball-final-four-matches |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Barringer-Grimes |first=Greg |date=July 14, 2026 |title=Why has the ball changed for World Cup semi-finals? |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/articles/c3wyw7pjv6qo |work=[[BBC Sport]] |accessdate=July 17, 2026 |archive-date=July 18, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260718070848/https://www.bbc.com/sport/articles/c3wyw7pjv6qo |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Venue==

===Stadium===
{{Main|MetLife Stadium}}[[File:MetLife Stadium Exterior, 2026 FIFA World Cup (June 20, 2026) (cropped).jpg|thumb|right|Exterior of MetLife Stadium as pictured in June 2026; its name was concealed to comply with FIFA's brand protection policy.<ref name="NYT-April17-Name">{{cite news |last=Mather |first=Victor |date=April 17, 2026 |title=New York New Jersey Stadium? Why MetLife Is Changing Its Name for the World Cup |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/17/nyregion/fifa-world-cup-stadium-names-sponsors.html |work=[[The New York Times]] |url-access=limited |accessdate=April 26, 2026}}</ref>]]

The final was played at MetLife Stadium, temporarily renamed New York New Jersey Stadium for the World Cup,<ref>{{cite news |last=Bowman |first=Emma |date=February 4, 2024 |title=The 2026 World Cup final will take place at New Jersey's MetLife Stadium |url=https://www.npr.org/2024/02/04/1229073496/2026-world-cup-final-nyc-nj-metlife-stadium |publisher=[[NPR]] |accessdate=February 7, 2024}}</ref><ref name="NYT-April17-Name" /> in [[East Rutherford, New Jersey]], {{convert|5|mi|km}} west of [[New York City]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Dominski |first=Michael |date=February 4, 2024 |title=World Cup 2026 schedule announcement live updates: Latest as FIFA selects host city for final |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/live-blogs/world-cup-2026-schedule-announcement-live-updates-final/r7oDr4oG9qK4/ |work=The Athletic |url-access=subscription |access-date=February 4, 2024 |archive-date=February 6, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240206103527/https://theathletic.com/live-blogs/world-cup-2026-schedule-announcement-live-updates-final/r7oDr4oG9qK4/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Over the tournament, the stadium hosted seven other matches, including five group stage matches, a round of 32 match, and a round of 16 match.<ref>{{Cite web |date=December 7, 2025 |title=2026 FIFA World Cup schedule at MetLife Stadium for New York-New Jersey matches |url=https://abc7ny.com/post/2026-world-cup-schedule-metlife-stadium/18259275/ |access-date=July 17, 2026 |website=ABC7 New York |language=en |archive-date=April 23, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260423021206/https://abc7ny.com/post/2026-world-cup-schedule-metlife-stadium/18259275/ |url-status=live }}</ref> MetLife Stadium, previously known as New Meadowlands Stadium until 2011, opened in 2010 and has a listed capacity of 82,500 seats.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Blum |first=Ronald |date=February 4, 2024 |title=2026 World Cup final will be played at MetLife Stadium in New Jersey |url=https://apnews.com/article/2026-world-cup-schedule-66c16c2e5f22d6c63ead069e0958ad97 |access-date=July 17, 2026 |website=AP News |language=en |archive-date=July 17, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260717194247/https://apnews.com/article/2026-world-cup-schedule-66c16c2e5f22d6c63ead069e0958ad97 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="NYTimes-NYNJ2">{{cite news |last=Waldstein |first=David |date=February 9, 2024 |title='New York New Jersey' Got the World Cup Final. Now Comes the Hard Part. |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2024/02/09/nyregion/new-york-new-jersey-world-cup-final.html |work=The New York Times |url-access=limited |accessdate=February 13, 2024 |archive-date=March 25, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250325232935/https://www.nytimes.com/2024/02/09/nyregion/new-york-new-jersey-world-cup-final.html |url-status=live }}</ref> It is home to the [[New York Giants]] and [[New York Jets]] of the [[National Football League]] (NFL) and had previously hosted [[Super Bowl XLVIII]] in 2014,<ref name="NYTimes-NYNJ2" /> the [[Copa América Centenario final]] in 2016,<ref>{{cite news |last1=Anzidei |first1=Melaine |last2=Rampling |first2=Ali |date=February 4, 2024 |title=New Jersey's MetLife Stadium wins the 2026 World Cup final |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5241468/2024/02/04/world-cup-final-2026-new-jersey/ |work=The Athletic |url-access=subscription |access-date=February 4, 2024 |archive-date=October 29, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251029154017/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5241468/2024/02/04/world-cup-final-2026-new-jersey/ |url-status=live }}</ref> and the [[2025 FIFA Club World Cup final]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Deen |first=Safid |date=September 28, 2024 |title=2025 FIFA Club World Cup final set: Where games will be played in U.S. |url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/sports/soccer/2024/09/28/fifa-club-world-cup-2025-game-sites/75434506007/ |work=USA Today |accessdate=October 14, 2024}}</ref>  

MetLife Stadium was proposed as the ideal final venue in the [[United 2026 FIFA World Cup bid|United 2026 bid]],<ref name="bid cities">{{cite web|url=https://digitalhub.fifa.com/m/3c077448dcd5c0ab/original/w3yjeu7dadt5erw26wmu-pdf.pdf|title=Canada, Mexico, and the United States United Bid to Host the 2026 FIFA World Cup™|publisher=united2026.com|access-date=April 30, 2018|archive-date=September 15, 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210915131958/https://digitalhub.fifa.com/m/3c077448dcd5c0ab/original/w3yjeu7dadt5erw26wmu-pdf.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> which was selected on June 13, 2018.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Panja|first1=Tariq|last2=Das|first2=Andrew|date=June 13, 2018|title=World Cup 2026: United States, Canada and Mexico Win Bid to Be Host|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/13/sports/world-cup/fifa-2026-vote-north-america-morocco.html|work=The New York Times|accessdate=January 18, 2024|archive-date=May 15, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200515074954/https://www.nytimes.com/2018/06/13/sports/world-cup/fifa-2026-vote-north-america-morocco.html|url-status=live}}</ref> The stadium was announced as the final venue in February 2024.<ref>{{cite news |last=Panja |first=Tariq |author-link=Tariq Panja |date=September 15, 2023 |title=FIFA Delays Have Cities Worried About 2026 World Cup Plans |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/09/15/sports/soccer/2026-world-cup.html |work=The New York Times |url-access=limited |accessdate=January 18, 2024 |archive-date=September 16, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240916151532/https://www.nytimes.com/2023/09/15/sports/soccer/2026-world-cup.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="AP-Stadium2">{{cite news |last=Blum |first=Ronald |date=February 4, 2024 |title=2026 World Cup final will be played at MetLife Stadium in New Jersey |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/soccer/2024/02/04/2026-world-cup-schedule/1c65f7a0-c3a0-11ee-bbc9-9b5ca9b20779_story.html |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |agency=Associated Press |accessdate=February 4, 2024}}</ref> The [[New Jersey Sports and Exposition Authority]] (NJSEA), which oversees MetLife Stadium, approved a $15.9 million contract to expand MetLife Stadium in June 2023, which was done to meet FIFA's size requirements.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Sobko |first=Katie |last2=Anzidei |first2=Melanie |date=June 23, 2023 |title=This is what the NJSEA will spend to renovate MetLife Stadium for FIFA World Cup Soccer |url=https://www.northjersey.com/story/sports/soccer/2023/06/23/fifa-world-cup-2026-metlife-stadium-cost-renovation-location/70347092007/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230703163919/https://eu.northjersey.com/story/sports/soccer/2023/06/23/fifa-world-cup-2026-metlife-stadium-cost-renovation-location/70347092007/ |archive-date=July 3, 2023 |access-date=July 22, 2026 |website=North Jersey Media Group |language=en-US}}</ref> In preparation for the World Cup, the stadium went under renovations in January 2024. Work was divided into two phases, completed in May 2024 and May 2025, during the NFL offseason.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Newcomb |first=Tim |date=April 23, 2024 |title=Skanska Reconfigures MetLife Stadium for World Cup |url=https://www.enr.com/articles/58514-skanska-reconfigures-metlife-stadium-for-world-cup |work=[[Engineering News-Record]] |access-date=July 17, 2026 |archive-date=July 18, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260718052836/https://www.enr.com/articles/58514-skanska-reconfigures-metlife-stadium-for-world-cup |url-status=live }}</ref> A total of 1,740 seats in the lower bowl corners were removed to widen the field;<ref>{{cite news |date=January 4, 2024 |title=MetLife Stadium to remove 1,740 seats in bid to host 2026 World Cup final |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2024/jan/04/metlife-stadium-world-cup-final-2026-seating |work=The Guardian |agency=Associated Press |accessdate=January 18, 2024}}</ref> several sections of removable [[bleacher]] seating were then installed for NFL games.<ref name=":3">{{cite news |last=McCormick |first=Bret |date=February 26, 2024 |title=Work already underway at MetLife Stadium ahead of 2026 World Cup |url=https://www.sportsbusinessjournal.com/Articles/2024/02/26/metlife-stadium-preparations-for-world-cup/ |work=[[Sports Business Journal]] |accessdate=July 17, 2026 |archive-date=May 19, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260519070431/https://www.sportsbusinessjournal.com/Articles/2024/02/26/metlife-stadium-preparations-for-world-cup/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Bushnell |first=Henry |date=February 3, 2026 |title=How NFL stadiums are transforming for the 2026 World Cup |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7007467/2026/02/03/2026-world-cup-nfl-stadiums/ |work=The Athletic |url-access=subscription |accessdate=July 17, 2026 |archive-date=February 3, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260203123014/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7007467/2026/02/03/2026-world-cup-nfl-stadiums/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In May 2026, a [[Bermuda grass]] surface, sourced from [[North Carolina]], was temporarily laid over the field's normal [[artificial turf]] for the tournament. The grass was originally supposed to be sourced from [[New Jersey]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Anzidei |first=Melanie |date=May 8, 2026 |title=Installing the World Cup final pitch: 27 trucks, a 12-hour journey and 10-hour shifts |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7263429/2026/05/08/new-york-new-jersey-world-cup-stadium-grass-turf-installation/ |work=The Athletic |url-access=subscription |access-date=July 5, 2026}}</ref> The pitch was later criticized by players, including [[Vinícius Júnior]] and [[Adrien Rabiot]], with them describing it as "dry and difficult", while visible worn patches were noted by the media.<ref>{{cite news |last=Battison |first=Paul |date=June 17, 2026 |title=Rabiot raises concerns about MetLife Stadium pitch |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cevld1m8nxno |work=BBC Sport |accessdate=July 5, 2026 |archive-date=July 16, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260716201524/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cevld1m8nxno |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Anzidei |first=Melanie |date=June 21, 2026 |title=Is MetLife Stadium's pitch fit to host the World Cup final? Players and coaches have concerns |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7377642/2026/06/21/metlife-pitch-world-cup-final-fifa/ |work=The Athletic |url-access=subscription |access-date=July 5, 2026 |archive-date=July 13, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260713160914/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7377642/2026/06/21/metlife-pitch-world-cup-final-fifa/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

During the tournament, the [[Meadowlands Rail Line]], a shuttle train with no regular service,<ref>{{cite news |last=Nessen |first=Stephen |date=February 6, 2024 |title=Is NJ Transit up for hosting the World Cup in 2026? Commuters are skeptical. |url=https://gothamist.com/news/nj-transit-world-cup-train-delays-2014-super-bowl |accessdate=February 13, 2024 |work=[[Gothamist]] |archive-date=October 21, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251021105344/https://gothamist.com/news/nj-transit-world-cup-train-delays-2014-super-bowl |url-status=live }}</ref> and a new $35&nbsp;million [[Bus lane|busway]] were planned to be used to transport attendees from the nearest transit hub at [[Secaucus Junction]].<ref name="AP-Stadium22">{{cite news |last=Blum |first=Ronald |date=February 4, 2024 |title=2026 World Cup final will be played at MetLife Stadium in New Jersey |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/sports/soccer/2024/02/04/2026-world-cup-schedule/1c65f7a0-c3a0-11ee-bbc9-9b5ca9b20779_story.html |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |agency=Associated Press |accessdate=February 4, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Higgs |first=Larry |date=February 6, 2024 |title=NJ Transit will operate 'on steroids' during the World Cup, Murphy says. Here's the plan. |url=https://www.nj.com/news/2024/02/nj-transit-will-operate-on-steroids-during-the-world-cup-murphy-says-heres-the-plan.html |work=[[The Star-Ledger]]|url-access=subscription |accessdate=February 13, 2024}}</ref> In April 2026, [[NJ Transit]], which operates the Meadowlands Rail Line, announced that $150 tickets would be issued for the train service, with dedicated queues at [[New York Penn Station]], and $80 fares for express buses. These costs, higher than the usual $12.90 train fare for MetLife Stadium services, were announced to offset the $48{{nbsp}}million cost of providing event service and security that was not subsidized by FIFA and other grants.<ref>{{cite news |last=Chen |first=Stefanos |date=April 17, 2026 |title=World Cup Transit Prices Are Set: $150 by Train and $80 for Shuttles |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2026/04/17/nyregion/nj-transit-world-cup-ticket-prices.html |work=The New York Times |url-access=limited |accessdate=April 26, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |date=April 17, 2026 |title=World Cup fans to MetLife Stadium will pay $150 train fares – NJ Transit |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/48514008/world-cup-fans-metlife-stadium-pay150-dollar-train-fares-nj-transit |publisher=ESPN |agency=Associated Press |accessdate=April 26, 2026}}</ref> In May 2026, NJ Transit announced that sponsorships and other funds raised would allow train fares to be reduced to $105 and later to $98.<ref>{{cite news |last=Enuco |first=Matthew |date=May 7, 2026 |title=NJ Transit reduces controversial World Cup ticket prices |url=https://www.nj.com/news/2026/05/nj-transit-reduces-controversial-world-cup-ticket-prices.html |work=The Star-Ledger |accessdate=May 7, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Crafton |first=Adam |date=June 2, 2026 |title=Less than six percent of $98 New Jersey Transit tickets to MetLife Stadium sold |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7325295/2026/06/02/metlife-stadium-world-cup-transport-tickets-unsold/ |work=The Athletic |url-access=subscription |access-date=July 5, 2026 |archive-date=June 13, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260613160936/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/7325295/2026/06/02/metlife-stadium-world-cup-transport-tickets-unsold/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

===Host selection===
{{Main|United 2026 FIFA World Cup bid}}
In August 2017, the United Bid Committee listed MetLife Stadium as among the 37 stadiums for consideration as potential host venues.<ref>{{Cite web |date=August 15, 2017 |title=UNITED BID COMMITTEE COMMENCES OUTREACH FOR POTENTIAL HOST CITIES IN BID FOR 2026 FIFA WORLD CUP |url=https://www.ussoccer.com/stories/2017/08/15/15/11/20170815-news-wc2026-united-bid-committee-commences-outreach-for-potential-host-cities |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170816060944/https://www.ussoccer.com/stories/2017/08/15/15/11/20170815-news-wc2026-united-bid-committee-commences-outreach-for-potential-host-cities |archive-date=August 16, 2017 |access-date=July 22, 2026 |website=www.ussoccer.com}}</ref> After the selection of the United 2026 bid in June 2018, MetLife Stadium was considered an early frontrunner to host the final, due to its proximity to New York City and experience of hosting major sporting events.<ref>{{cite news |last=Goff|first=Steven|author-link=Steven Goff|date=June 16, 2018|title=2026 World Cup host cities: Ranking the contenders|url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/soccer-insider/wp/2018/06/16/2026-world-cup-host-cities-ranking-the-contenders/|newspaper=The Washington Post|accessdate=January 18, 2024|archive-date=October 25, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221025004801/https://www.washingtonpost.com/news/soccer-insider/wp/2018/06/16/2026-world-cup-host-cities-ranking-the-contenders/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Conway|first=Richard|date=June 13, 2018|title=World Cup 2026: How US, Canada & Mexico won right to host tournament|url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44465069|work=BBC Sport|accessdate=January 18, 2024|archive-date=June 3, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260603131806/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/44465069|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=":4">{{cite news |last=Sherman|first=Ted|date=September 23, 2021|title=Will MetLife Stadium be home to the 2026 World Cup finals? N.Y. and N.J. hope to score a slot with FIFA.|url=https://www.nj.com/news/2021/09/will-metlife-stadium-be-home-to-the-2026-world-cup-finals-ny-and-nj-hope-to-score-a-slot-with-fifa.html|work=[[The Star-Ledger]]|accessdate=January 18, 2024|archive-date=October 26, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251026211013/https://www.nj.com/news/2021/09/will-metlife-stadium-be-home-to-the-2026-world-cup-finals-ny-and-nj-hope-to-score-a-slot-with-fifa.html|url-status=live}}</ref> As a part of FIFA's Candidate Host City tour for the World Cup, MetLife Stadium and its facilities were visited by FIFA representatives in September 2021.<ref name=":4" /> That year, a joint bid—formed by New York and New Jersey—was submitted for MetLife Stadium to host multiple matches during the World Cup.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 16, 2022 |title=New York / New Jersey Selected as Host City for 2026 FIFA World Cup™ |url=https://www.nyc.gov/mayors-office/news/2022/06/new-york-new-jersey-selected-host-city-2026-fifa-world-cup |access-date=July 22, 2026 |website=Official Website of New York City Government |language=en}}</ref> In June 2022, the stadium was announced as among of the sixteen host venues, including its competitor [[SoFi Stadium]], a newer stadium in [[Inglewood, California]], near [[Los Angeles]].<ref>{{cite news |date=June 16, 2022|title=World Cup 2026 host cities confirmed: What you need to know about the 16 venues|url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/37628821/need-know-16-venues|publisher=ESPN|accessdate=January 18, 2024|archive-date=December 5, 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231205030255/https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/37628821/need-know-16-venues|url-status=live}}</ref> The [[Rose Bowl (stadium)|Rose Bowl]] in [[Pasadena, California]], the Los Angeles area venue that hosted the [[1994 FIFA World Cup final|1994 men's final]] and [[1999 FIFA Women's World Cup final|1999 women's final]], was not chosen for the tournament due to its age.<ref>{{cite news |date=June 16, 2022 |title=World Cup 2026 host cities confirmed: What you need to know about the 16 venues |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/37628821/need-know-16-venues |publisher=ESPN |accessdate=January 18, 2024 |archive-date=December 5, 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20231205030255/https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/37628821/need-know-16-venues |url-status=live }}</ref>

SoFi Stadium was designed primarily for [[American football]] with a field that is {{convert|69|yd|m}} wide—narrower than FIFA's recommended dimensions.<ref>{{cite news |last=Baxter |first=Kevin |date=August 3, 2022 |title=Leagues Cup friendlies showing L.A. is a soccer market |url=https://www.latimes.com/sports/soccer/story/2022-08-03/galaxy-chivas-de-guadalajara-lafc-club-america-recap-leagues-cup |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |accessdate=January 18, 2024 |archive-date=December 14, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251214021847/https://www.latimes.com/sports/soccer/story/2022-08-03/galaxy-chivas-de-guadalajara-lafc-club-america-recap-leagues-cup |url-status=live }}</ref> According to media reports, the stadium's owner, [[Kroenke Sports & Entertainment]], was also unhappy with the revenue-sharing deal proposed by FIFA and threatened to cancel their plans to host World Cup matches.<ref>{{cite news |last=Crafton |first=Adam |date=September 21, 2023 |title=Kroenkes in dispute with FIFA over SoFi Stadium deal for 2026 World Cup |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/4882751/2023/09/21/world-cup-2026-kroenkes-sofi-fifa/ |work=[[The Athletic]] |url-access=subscription |access-date=January 18, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Bushnell |first=Henry |date=November 2, 2023 |title=LA-FIFA standoff at center of 2026 World Cup's first battle: Who's paying for it? |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/2026-world-cup-fifa-los-angeles-standoff-185240509.html |work=[[Yahoo! Sports]] |accessdate=January 18, 2024 |archive-date=December 26, 2024 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241226015018/https://sports.yahoo.com/2026-world-cup-fifa-los-angeles-standoff-185240509.html |url-status=live }}</ref> In early 2023, [[AT&T Stadium]] in the [[Dallas]] area emerged as a potential host for the final due to its higher capacity of 90,000 seats and scheduled renovations to accommodate a wider field.<ref>{{cite news |last=Nudelstejer |first=Abraham |date=March 15, 2023 |title=FIFA delegation visits AT&T Stadium to offer insight on World Cup-prompted renovations |url=https://www.dallasnews.com/sports/fc-dallas/2023/03/15/fifa-delegation-visits-att-stadium-to-offer-insight-on-world-cup-prompted-renovations/ |work=[[Dallas Morning News]] |accessdate=January 18, 2024}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Wilen |first=Holden |date=January 18, 2024 |title=Some think DFW has already secured 2026 World Cup final |url=https://www.bizjournals.com/dallas/news/2024/01/18/fifa-world-cup-final-possibly-in-dallas-arlington.html |work=[[Dallas Business Journal]] |accessdate=January 18, 2024 |archive-date=October 20, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251020140846/https://www.bizjournals.com/dallas/news/2024/01/18/fifa-world-cup-final-possibly-in-dallas-arlington.html |url-status=live }}</ref> According to ''[[The Athletic]]'', the selection of MetLife Stadium came as a "surprise" to local officials, who organized a small viewing party for the announcement. Dallas's bid had been favored—and rumored as the winner in January—and included a simulcast of the match at two nearby venues to increase ticket revenue.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Anzidei |first1=Melanie |last2=Crafton |first2=Adam |date=February 5, 2024 |title=Inside the World Cup 2026 final host city selection: Why New Jersey was picked over Dallas |url=https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5252093/2024/02/05/world-cup-2026-final-metlife-stadium-selection-process/ |work=The Athletic |url-access=subscription |access-date=February 7, 2024 |archive-date=July 20, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250720170359/https://www.nytimes.com/athletic/5252093/2024/02/05/world-cup-2026-final-metlife-stadium-selection-process/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

