|}

Spain entered the 2026 World Cup as a favorite to win the tournament, and qualified after securing first place in their [[2026 FIFA World Cup qualification – UEFA Group E|qualification group]].<ref name="CNN-CPV">{{cite news |last1=Feldscher |first1=Kyle |last2=Riddell |first2=Don |date=June 15, 2026 |title=Tiny Cape Verde shocks favorite Spain with a draw in country's first World Cup match |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/06/15/sport/cape-verde-spain-world-cup |publisher=[[CNN]] |accessdate=July 14, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Kallas |first=Fernando |date=November 18, 2025 |title=Spain qualify for World Cup after 2–2 draw with Turkey |url=https://www.reuters.com/sports/soccer/spain-qualify-world-cup-after-2-2-draw-with-turkey-2025-11-18/ |work=Reuters |access-date=July 17, 2026}}</ref> They were drawn into [[2026 FIFA World Cup Group H|Group H]], alongside [[Cape Verde national football team|Cape Verde]], [[Saudi Arabia national football team|Saudi Arabia]], and [[Uruguay national football team|Uruguay]]. Their first match was against World Cup debutants Cape Verde on June 15 at [[Mercedes-Benz Stadium]] in [[Atlanta]]; the game ended in a 0–0 draw, due to a strong defensive performance from Cape Verde and their goalkeeper, [[Vozinha]].<ref name="CNN-CPV"/> Their second match was against Saudi Arabia in Atlanta on June 21. Spain took the lead early when [[Mikel Oyarzabal]] [[Cross (association football)|crossed]] from the left and [[Lamine Yamal]] scored from close range. This was followed by a [[corner kick]] for Spain, which Oyarzabal scored to double their lead in the 21st minute. Three minutes later, Oyarzabal scored his second from close range to give Spain a 3–0 lead. In the second half, [[Marc Cucurella]] took the kick from the corner, in which Saudi Arabia's goalkeeper [[Hassan Tambakti]] saved the ball, but it deflected off [[Hassan Al-Tambakti|Hassan Al Tambakti]] into the goal for an [[own goal]], which secured Spain a 4–0 victory.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Brewin |first=John |date=June 21, 2026 |title=Spain 4-0 Saudi Arabia: World Cup 2026 – as it happened |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2026/jun/21/spain-v-saudi-arabia-world-cup-2026-live |access-date=July 23, 2026 |work=The Guardian |language=en-GB |issn=0261-3077}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Lowe |first=Sid |author-link=Sid Lowe |date=June 21, 2026 |title=Lamine Yamal and Oyarzabal fire Spain to emphatic win against Saudi Arabia |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jun/21/spain-saudi-arabia-world-cup-group-h-match-report |work=[[The Guardian]] |accessdate=July 14, 2026}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Howarth |first=Matthew |date=June 21, 2026 |title=Spain vs Saudi Arabia LIVE – June 21, 2026 |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/c1eyg6lq5wvt |access-date=July 23, 2026 |website=BBC Sport |language=en-GB}}</ref> Spain finished atop Group H with seven points after a 1–0 win over Uruguay in their third match, played June 26 at [[Estadio Akron]] near [[Guadalajara]], with a goal from [[Álex Baena]] minutes before halftime.<ref>{{cite news |last=Azzoni |first=Tales |date=June 27, 2026 |title=Spain wins its World Cup group, beating Uruguay as Muslera's error sends 2-time champions home |url=https://www.cbc.ca/sports/soccer/worldcup/fifa-world-cup-uruguay-spain-recap-june-26-9.7248713 |work=CBC Sports |agency=Associated Press |accessdate=July 14, 2026 |archive-date=July 15, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260715045446/https://www.cbc.ca/sports/soccer/worldcup/fifa-world-cup-uruguay-spain-recap-june-26-9.7248713 |url-status=live }}</ref>

As group winners, Spain played [[2026 FIFA World Cup Group J|Group J]] runners-up [[Austria national football team|Austria]] in the [[2026 FIFA World Cup round of 32|round of 32]] on July 2 at [[SoFi Stadium]] in the [[Los Angeles]] area. The team won 3–0 with a pair of goals from Oyarzabal and a header by right-back [[Pedro Porro]] to beat Austria goalkeeper [[Alexander Schlager]], who made several key saves in the first half. The performance, described as "dominant" by [[BBC Sport]], was Spain's first win in a World Cup knockout stage since the [[2010 FIFA World Cup final|2010 final]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Smith |first=Emma |date=July 2, 2026 |title=Spain 3–0 Austria: Mikel Oyarzabal scores twice in dominant win |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/c8j2wvnzkvzt |work=BBC Sport |accessdate=July 14, 2026 |archive-date=July 4, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260704122957/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/c8j2wvnzkvzt |url-status=live }}</ref> The team advanced to the round of 16 to play [[Portugal national football team|Portugal]] on July 6 at [[AT&T Stadium]] near [[Dallas]]. The Spanish attack were given few clear chances to score, but won 1–0 with a stoppage time goal from substitute [[Mikel Merino]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Wilson |first=Jonathan |date=July 6, 2026 |title=Merino strikes at the death for Spain to send Portugal and Ronaldo limping out |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2026/jul/06/portugal-spain-world-cup-last-16-match-report |work=The Guardian |accessdate=July 14, 2026}}</ref> Spain returned to the Los Angeles area for the quarterfinal against [[Belgium national football team|Belgium]] on July 10. The two sides exchanged goals in the first half, with [[Fabián Ruiz]] giving Spain the lead in the 30th minute through a rebound off goalkeeper [[Thibaut Courtois]] and Belgium's [[Charles De Ketelaere]] equalizing with a header in the 41st minute. Courtois made several saves but left the match with an injury in the second half; his replacement, [[Senne Lammens]], was unable to recover a rebound that fell to Merino's feet to score in the 88th minute and earn a 2–1 victory.<ref>{{cite news |last=Baxter |first=Kevin |date=July 10, 2026 |title=Mikel Merino lifts Spain over Belgium, setting up World Cup showdown with France |url=https://www.latimes.com/sports/soccer/story/2026-07-10/spain-defeats-belgium-will-face-france-in-world-cup-semi-finals |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |accessdate=July 14, 2026}}</ref> 

[[File:Spain Starting XI France v Spain 7.24.26-029.jpg|thumb|left|upright=0.87|Spain's starting lineup against France in the semifinals.]]
In the semifinals, Spain faced another tournament favorite, [[France national football team|France]], on July 14 in Dallas; France had outscored opponents 16–2 in their previous six matches and maintained [[clean sheet]]s in four matches.<ref name="Yahoo-FRA">{{cite news |last=Goff |first=Steven |author-link=Steven Goff |date=July 14, 2026 |title=Spain storms into World Cup final after shutting down France |url=https://sports.yahoo.com/soccer/live/france-vs-spain-world-cup-2026-scores-results-schedule-live-updates-150000075.html |work=[[Yahoo Sports]] |accessdate=July 14, 2026 |archive-date=July 14, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260714190852/https://sports.yahoo.com/soccer/live/france-vs-spain-world-cup-2026-scores-results-schedule-live-updates-150000075.html |url-status=live }}</ref> By the semifinal, the team had traveled over {{convert|12000|mi|km}} between its [[2026 FIFA World Cup base camps|base camp]] in [[Chattanooga, Tennessee]], and matches played in Mexico and the United States.<ref>{{cite news |last1=Moallim |first1=Mohamed |last2=Bradshaw |first2=Joe |date=July 13, 2026 |title=England top air miles among semi-finalists – does it matter? |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cj4g7v9e51jo |work=BBC Sport |accessdate=July 14, 2026 |archive-date=July 13, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260713164706/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/articles/cj4g7v9e51jo |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Snell |first=Patrick |date=June 5, 2026 |title=Inside the Tennessee high school that's Spain's home away from home at this year's World Cup |url=https://www.cnn.com/2026/06/05/sport/spain-world-cup-base-tennessee |publisher=CNN |accessdate=July 14, 2026}}</ref> Spain's strong defensive performance, led by [[Rodri (footballer, born 1996)|Rodri]] in the midfield, withstood France's attack and produced openings that were exploited. A [[penalty kick (association football)|penalty kick]] won by Lamine Yamal was converted by Oyarzabal in the 22nd minute and was followed by a one-two play with [[Dani Olmo]] to Pedro Porro to score in the 58th minute. Spain won 2–0 and booked their place in the final.<ref name="Yahoo-FRA" /><ref>{{cite news |last=Drury |first=Sam |date=July 14, 2026 |title=Superb Spain beat France to reach World Cup final |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cj3gdp4d3nnt#Report |work=BBC Sport |accessdate=July 14, 2026 |archive-date=July 14, 2026 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260714175419/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cj3gdp4d3nnt#Report |url-status=live }}</ref>

